BoosterTutor is a coach for Magic: the Gathering Limited. You give it a draft or a
sealed pool, and it tells you which picks cost you equity and why — not as a
scoreboard, but as a paragraph you could hear from a person sitting next to you.

The architecture rests on one rule, written into the project's `CONTEXT.md` on the
first day: **Python computes every fact and every verdict; the LLM only explains
them in prose.** That constraint is the whole design. A language model asked to
grade a pick will produce a confident number it cannot justify; a language model
handed a number and asked to explain it stays useful and stays honest.

## Grading a pick

Every card gets a *fitness* — its value to a specific pool at a specific moment,
measured in percentage points of win rate, using [17lands](https://www.17lands.com)
data as the underlying signal. Because every term lands on one interpretable scale,
"this card was worth 2pp less to your deck" is a sentence a coach can actually say.

Picks are then graded on the **gap** to the best card in the pack, not on rank.
Most picks are close, and flagging every non-optimal one would bury the three that
mattered under thirty that did not. The bands are fixed: `solid` within 2pp,
`questionable` within 4pp, `mistake` beyond. A pack where nothing was at stake —
one playable, nothing scorable, or every card already `solid` — is `indifferent`
rather than graded, because no available choice cost anything.

Most of the difficulty lives in deciding what a pool is *trying* to become. Ten
two-colour archetypes are scored as competing hypotheses, each with a float
describing what share of the pool it could play, and a card's value depends on the
commitment it asks for as much as on its raw strength.

## Building it

The API is FastAPI and SQLAlchemy over Postgres, with Alembic migrations, `uv` for
packaging, and mypy, ruff and pytest in the loop. The web client is SvelteKit and
Tailwind, tested with Vitest in a real browser through Playwright.

The part worth pointing at is not the stack, though — it is `docs/decisions/`.
Sixteen ADRs record how the scoring evolved: the fitness function itself, the
synergy term, the decay coefficient, and one full rewrite of the classification
system after the original conflated two questions ("what did this pick cost?" and
"is this card any good?") into a single label and produced results that read as
noise.

